WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:05.700
Why so serious? Because this fan-favorite&nbsp;
performance in "John Wick: Chapter 4" was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:08.640
channeling none other than&nbsp;
Heath Ledger – no kidding.

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:12.600
The fourth "John Wick" movie has blown&nbsp;
away the box office and earned some of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:17.280
the best reviews of the entire franchise. While&nbsp;
a lot of praise rightfully goes toward the lead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:23.577
Keanu Reeves and director Chad Stahelski, the film&nbsp;
introduces many exciting new characters that could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.577 --> 00:00:29.220
easily spin off into their own franchises,&nbsp;
including The Tracker, a.k.a. Mr. Nobody.

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:34.440
True to his name, Mr. Nobody is a blank-slate&nbsp;
bounty hunter who's only interested in taking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:38.940
out John Wick if he can get as much money&nbsp;
out of it as possible. He aligns himself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:42.720
with various forces throughout the&nbsp;
movie, along with his trusted canine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:46.800
putting him in a very interesting position&nbsp;
by the end of the film. And there are many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:50.940
directions he can go because there's still&nbsp;
a lot the audience doesn't know about him.

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:56.460
That's intentional, and actor Shamier Anderson&nbsp;
looked toward another mysterious antagonist for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.460 --> 00:01:00.720
inspiration — Heath Ledger's Joker in&nbsp;
"The Dark Knight." As he told Vulture,

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:05.340
"I looked at the Joker and studied a lot of what&nbsp;
Heath Ledger did, how exciting that character was;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:10.560
you just knew that guy had a robust past.&nbsp;
Like, Why did he get those scars? And, for me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:15.060
Why do I have this canine? Why do I have&nbsp;
this knapsack? Why am I dressed like this?"

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:20.000
The result is a thrilling new character that&nbsp;
should absolutely get more time on the big screen.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:22.940
When an actor's trying to build&nbsp;
a bad guy from the ground up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:27.360
drawing inspiration from one of the best movie&nbsp;
villains of all time is an excellent place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:31.260
to start. There's also much the audience&nbsp;
doesn't know about Heath Ledger's Joker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:35.520
and that makes him all the more terrifying.&nbsp;
Shamier Anderson took what was accessible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:39.180
about his character and fleshed him&nbsp;
out from there, going on to state,

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:42.720
"This guy's transient. He has&nbsp;
pockets because he travels so much;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:46.920
he doesn't really change. That notebook you&nbsp;
see in the film, I took that home and wrote&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:51.720
a lot in there. That knapsack that he had on,&nbsp;
I had a lot of personal items, like dog toys,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:56.624
dog treats, lighters engraved with my&nbsp;
name. I just had to put my DNA on this."

00:01:56.624 --> 00:02:00.660
The part about writing in the notebook&nbsp;
seen in the film is an interesting parallel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:06.000
to Heath Ledger's preparation to play Joker. The&nbsp;
actor infamously kept a diary to jot down his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:10.484
character's villainous thoughts during production,&nbsp;
including lines of dialogue he had in the film.

00:02:10.981 --> 00:02:16.984
"You look nervous. Is it the&nbsp;
scars? Wanna know how I got 'em?"

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:21.900
All of Anderson's hard work appears to have&nbsp;
paid off. He concluded his comments by saying,

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:25.420
"I think it's translating as people&nbsp;
are infatuated with the Tracker."

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:28.800
In a film like "John Wick: Chapter&nbsp;
4," which has many highlights,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:33.843
being one of the best parts is definitely a&nbsp;
compliment and a testament to Anderson's talents.

00:02:33.843 --> 00:02:37.796
He may have channeled Heath Ledger's Joker&nbsp;
for inspiration in "John Wick: Chapter 4,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.796 --> 00:02:42.240
but Anderson is also a fan of another actor who&nbsp;
played a villain in the "Dark Knight" franchise.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:47.160
In that same interview with Vulture, Anderson&nbsp;
said that he'd love to star in a Mr. Nobody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:52.308
spinoff with Liam Neeson — who played Ra's Al Ghul&nbsp;
in "Batman Begins" and "The Dark Knight Rises."

00:02:52.308 --> 00:02:56.280
And he's not the only performer Anderson&nbsp;
would be interested in walking the boards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.280 --> 00:02:59.880
with. Speaking of people he'd love&nbsp;
to go up against in movies, he said,

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:04.380
"Liam Neeson would be a great antagonist.&nbsp;
I think Denzel Washington would be a crazy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:09.120
antagonist. It would be really interesting to see&nbsp;
a female villain; Meryl Streep would be something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:14.340
different. So those are my top three choices:&nbsp;
Denzel Washington, Liam Neeson, and Meryl Streep."

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:18.240
It remains to be seen if Mr. Nobody will&nbsp;
ever get his own spin-off. In the meantime,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:21.833
fans can look forward to returning&nbsp;
to the "John Wick" universe via "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.833 --> 00:03:24.892
Continental" and "Ballerina"&nbsp;
spin-offs in the near future.

